<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aghlabid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/800">800</a>–909</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Aghlabids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aghlabid dynasty ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, roughly present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> as hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> under nominal Abbasid authority from 800 to 909. In 827 they began the long conquest of Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, which created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Sicily. They built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a> in its present form and were overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> in 909.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Aghlabid dynasty" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Nanoxyde at French Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aghlabids_Dynasty_800_-_909_(AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He did so partly to distance himself from the Malikite jurists and theologians, who condemned the luxurious life of the <b>Aghlabids</b>, disliked their Mu'tazilite theology and Hanafi law, and objected to the unequal treatment of the Muslim Berbers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 909 his Kutama army overthrew the <b>Aghlabid</b> emirate, and the imam was retrieved from Sijilmasa and proclaimed caliph at Raqqada in January 910 as Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah, establishing a rival caliphate to Baghdad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Idris II broke that dependence by welcoming Arab settlers at Walili and appointing Arabs as his vizier and qadi; when the Awraba leader Ishak plotted against his life with the <b>Aghlabids</b> of Tunisia, Idris had his former protector killed and in 809 left Walili for Fez.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After a siege, Melite, the capital of Malta, the last island around Sicily in Byzantine hands, fell to the <b>Aghlabids</b>, reinforced by a fleet sent from Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-29">29 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Aghlabids</b> had begun their conquest of Byzantine Sicily in 827.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-21">21 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 827 the <b>Aghlabids of Ifriqiya</b> invaded Sicily at the invitation of the rebel Byzantine commander Euphemius.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily</a></li></ul></section>
